  <HOMEWORK title="Homework 14: The eye looks fine, BER isn't" prompt="This link's eye diagram looks reasonably open and the receiver is equalizing normally, but BER testing still shows a measurable error rate that the eye alone doesn't explain. The bench next to this one was recently set up with a board that uses a common 100 MHz reference oscillator. Is that actually related, or a coincidence?"
            hint="Add a Jitter / Phase Noise Scope to the chain and look at its spectral density plot. Is there a narrow spike standing out above the noise floor - and if so, what frequency is it at?"
            explanation="The transmitted signal has a strong sinusoidal jitter component at exactly 100 MHz offset - the same frequency as that neighbouring board's reference oscillator. A single, specific spectral line like this is invisible as a distinguishable root cause from the eye diagram alone: the eye just looks a little softer than expected, which could be blamed on dozens of things. The Jitter / Phase Noise Scope's spectrum is the right tool here - a narrow, isolated line (not a broad, flat noise floor) is the signature of a specific external aggressor, not generic random jitter, and its exact frequency is often enough to identify the culprit by matching it against known clocks and switching frequencies nearby, exactly like this 100 MHz coincidence. There's no setting to flip here - the fix is physical (shielding, spacing, or a guard trace), but finding it starts with recognising that spike and reading off its frequency, not guessing from the eye."/>
</LABCHAIN>
